# _redirects — host-level 301s that do NOT belong in redirects.mjs.
#
# The @astrojs/cloudflare adapter reads this file at build and APPENDS the
# redirects.mjs-derived 301s after it (see the adapter's `astro:build:done`), so
# this stays the single _redirects source — redirects.mjs is not a separate list,
# it's merged in. Matching is top-to-bottom, first match wins.
#
# The HOST does not read this file: scripts/sync-amplify-redirects.mjs parses
# the merged dist/client/_redirects after each build and pushes the complete
# rule set to Amplify as app-level custom rules. Author redirects HERE (or in
# redirects.mjs), never in the AWS console — the sync overwrites console edits.
# Query-string matching is NOT supported by this format — that's why /?p=
# shortlinks can't live here.
#
# This file is the home for the two redirect classes redirects.mjs can't/shouldn't
# express:
#   1. Wildcards / splats — no static meta-refresh page can be generated per
#      unknown path.
#   2. Machine resource endpoints (feeds, sitemaps) — consumers follow the HTTP
#      301 but ignore the HTML meta-refresh fallback that redirects.mjs entries
#      emit, so a meta-refresh `.xml` file would be useless/broken for them.
# Human-navigable PAGE redirects stay in redirects.mjs (and also get the portable
# static fallback). Keep this list small and structural.
#
# Intentionally NOT redirected (left to 404 — correct behavior):
#   /wp-admin/*, /wp-json/*  — WP app/API surface; we are not a WordPress site.
#   /wp-content/uploads/*    — asset URLs; blog media was localized, and
#                              redirecting an <img> src to an HTML page is wrong.
#   /?p=<id> /?page_id=<id>  — WP shortlinks; WP already 301'd these to the pretty
#                              permalink, so they're rarely the canonical inbound
#                              link. Generatable later from each post's legacyId
#                              frontmatter (the WP post ID) if analytics show real
#                              hits.

# --- WordPress feed endpoints → the single Astro RSS feed (/rss.xml) ---
# Live serves these 200 today; feed readers follow the 301 and update their URL.
# Per-taxonomy feeds (/blog/category/<c>/feed/, /blog/tag/<t>/feed/) are lower
# value and left uncovered for now — add if needed.
/feed/            /rss.xml            301
/blog/feed/       /rss.xml            301
/comments/feed/   /rss.xml            301

# --- WordPress / Yoast sitemap URLs → the @astrojs/sitemap index ---
# Filename differs: WP used `/sitemap_index.xml` (underscore) — what search
# engines have on file — while @astrojs/sitemap emits `/sitemap-index.xml`
# (hyphen). Redirect both indexed WP URLs so they don't 404 after cutover.
/wp-sitemap.xml     /sitemap-index.xml   301
/sitemap_index.xml  /sitemap-index.xml   301
# Yoast's CHILD sitemaps. Google fetches these directly — it has each one on
# file from crawling the index, and keeps requesting them after the index
# itself has moved — so the parent redirect above does not cover them. These
# are exactly the 7 children live's /sitemap_index.xml lists (verified against
# openmined.org: 2026-08-12); all 7 404'd on the Worker before this block.
/post-sitemap.xml           /sitemap-index.xml   301
/page-sitemap.xml           /sitemap-index.xml   301
/category-sitemap.xml       /sitemap-index.xml   301
/post_tag-sitemap.xml       /sitemap-index.xml   301
/locations-sitemap.xml      /sitemap-index.xml   301
/resources-sitemap.xml      /sitemap-index.xml   301
/resource_type-sitemap.xml  /sitemap-index.xml   301

# --- Retired bare WordPress author base ---
# 404s on live today; the real author archives live at /blog/author/<slug>/
# (which the rebuild will serve as pages — do NOT redirect that prefix here).
/author/*         /blog/              301
